WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.611 --> 00:00:02.290
- [Cleve] In many, many
different cultures,

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:06.457
the rainbow appears as a
symbol of hope, beauty, love.

00:00:08.109 --> 00:00:09.309
In many ways the rainbow flag

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:12.142
might be more important than ever.

00:00:15.189 --> 00:00:17.120
- [Narrator] Our story
begins in San Francisco

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:18.640
in the late 1960s.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.371
- [Cleve] Young gay men,
lesbian women were arriving

00:00:21.371 --> 00:00:23.952
from all over the country
and all around the world,

00:00:23.952 --> 00:00:26.083
many of them fleeing
places where they were

00:00:26.083 --> 00:00:30.251
experiencing incredible
violence and repression.

00:00:30.251 --> 00:00:32.419
- [Narrator] One of those
men was Gilbert Baker.

00:00:32.419 --> 00:00:34.491
- [Cleve] Everyone was aware of Gilbert.

00:00:34.491 --> 00:00:36.219
He was extremely dramatic.

00:00:36.219 --> 00:00:38.560
I think the operative
word would be flamboyant.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:39.829
- [Narrator] That's Cleve Jones.

00:00:39.829 --> 00:00:40.739
He wrote this book.

00:00:40.739 --> 00:00:43.538
- [Cleve] When We Rise,
My Life in the Movement.

00:00:43.538 --> 00:00:45.938
- [Narrator] Cleve and Gilbert
met as two young activists

00:00:45.938 --> 00:00:49.429
planning the 1977 San
Francisco Gay Pride event.

00:00:49.429 --> 00:00:51.400
- [Cleve] Back then it was
called Gay Freedom Day.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:54.371
We were all part of a circle of activists

00:00:54.371 --> 00:00:57.309
who were talking about
the need for a new symbol

00:00:57.309 --> 00:00:58.920
that would connect us all.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.549
- [Narrator] And one
day, Gilbert told Cleve

00:01:00.549 --> 00:01:02.467
about an idea he had come up with.

00:01:02.467 --> 00:01:04.029
- [Cleve] He showed me
the drawings he'd done,

00:01:04.029 --> 00:01:06.459
and I saw the power of it right away.

00:01:06.459 --> 00:01:08.219
- [Narrator] And each color
had special significance.

00:01:08.219 --> 00:01:09.989
One color meant love, another was--

00:01:09.989 --> 00:01:11.936
- [Cleve] Honestly I think
he kind of made that up

00:01:11.936 --> 00:01:13.059
after the fact.

00:01:13.059 --> 00:01:16.538
It came to him as he said "from the sky."

00:01:16.538 --> 00:01:18.487
- [Narrator] With a team of
volunteers including Cleve,

00:01:18.487 --> 00:01:20.709
Gilbert sewed and dyed
the flags in preparation

00:01:20.709 --> 00:01:22.376
for Gay Freedom Day.

00:01:24.018 --> 00:01:24.851
- [Cleve] When those first flags

00:01:24.851 --> 00:01:27.087
went up these enormous poles,

00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:29.087
and the wind took them and lifted them up

00:01:29.087 --> 00:01:32.389
against the blue sky, it
was really breathtaking.

00:01:32.389 --> 00:01:34.578
Gilbert and I stood there
and watched and we knew

00:01:34.578 --> 00:01:36.439
that everybody that was there

00:01:36.439 --> 00:01:38.269
was looking up at those
rainbows and knowing

00:01:38.269 --> 00:01:42.149
at that exact moment,
this now would be our symbol.

00:01:42.149 --> 00:01:45.426
To take this beautiful symbol from nature

00:01:45.426 --> 00:01:47.590
and say this is the symbol of these people

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:49.378
who were called unnatural,

00:01:49.378 --> 00:01:51.870
who were required to live in the shadows,

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:55.068
he really devoted his life
not only to making flags

00:01:55.068 --> 00:01:56.068
but banners.

